{{!-- ENCOUNTERS — what has been gathered for one session or one scene, in named bundles: the
      monsters, the read-aloud page, the battle map, the treasure table, the arcanum somebody is
      about to find. The one tab on this sheet that holds POINTERS to other documents rather than
      prose about them.

      WRITES DO NOT GO THROUGH THE FORM. Every field here is handled by gm-encounters-tab.js and
      saved straight to `actor.system.encounters`, so none of them carries a `name` attribute: a
      `name` on an input inside this sheet's <form> puts it in core's submit data, where the array
      indices would expand into an OBJECT keyed "0", "1", ... and quietly replace the list with it.

      THE DRAG HANDLE IS THE NAME BUTTON, NOT THE ROW. In Chromium a `draggable` ancestor takes the
      mousedown from every input inside it, so a draggable <li> would leave the per-entry note box
      unselectable — you could not put a caret in it. Narrowing the handle to the icon-and-name
      control puts the note outside the draggable subtree. The button carries `data-uuid` and
      `data-doc-type` because the payload it emits is CORE'S OWN `{type, uuid}`: the same thing the
      sidebar puts on the wire, so dragging a monster out of here onto the canvas behaves exactly
      like dragging it out of the sidebar. An unresolved row is `draggable="false"` — there is
      nothing on the other end of it to drag.

      THERE IS NO <img> ANYWHERE ON THIS TAB, which is why nothing here carries the
      `draggable="false"` opt-out that every portrait in the system does (see
      tests/actors/steading/steading-actor-drag.test.js). Add a portrait and it needs one, or Gecko
      hands over the image instead of the document.

      EXPANDING IS A CLASS, NOT A RENDER. The body is emitted for every encounter and hidden by
      `.is-collapsed`, so the caret toggles it in place: instant, and incapable of landing a
      re-render on a note box two rows down that happens to have focus.

      ONE PENCIL for the whole tab, in the panel's corner, and it guards exactly the two delete
      buttons. Everything else stays live while it is off, which is the point rather than an
      oversight: the reason this tab exists is to be dropped into and noted on mid-session, and a
      tab that has to be unlocked first is one nobody uses. What the pencil is FOR is that "delete
      this encounter" sits two pixels from "mark it used", which is a button a GM presses
      constantly.

      NOT `stonetop-readonly`. That class is the sheet-wide lock (pointer-events off every input,
      textarea and button, then an allow-list), and on this tab it would put out exactly the fields
      the paragraph above says have to stay lit. `--reading` hides the two buttons it is about and
      touches nothing else.

      `data-tab="encounters"` earns the rail's stack glyph; the icon table in stonetop.css is one
      flat list keyed by data-tab with no per-sheet scope. --}}
<div class="tab encounters steading-edit-section" data-group="primary" data-tab="encounters">
	{{> "stonetop.steading-section-toggle" section="encounters" editing=stonetop.edit.encounters}}
	<section class="sheet-tab stonetop-gm-encounters{{#unless stonetop.edit.encounters}} stonetop-gm-encounters--reading{{/unless}}">

		<p class="steading-threats-intro">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.intro"}}</p>

		{{#if stonetop.encounters.length}}
		<ul class="stonetop-gm-encounter-list">
			{{#each stonetop.encounters}}
			<li class="stonetop-gm-encounter{{#if used}} is-used{{/if}}{{#unless open}} is-collapsed{{/unless}}"
			    data-encounter-id="{{id}}">
				<div class="stonetop-gm-encounter-head">
					<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-grip" draggable="true" aria-hidden="true"
					      data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.reorder"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
						<i class="fas fa-grip-vertical"></i>
					</span>
					<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-toggle" aria-expanded="{{#if open}}true{{else}}false{{/if}}"
					        aria-label="{{#if open}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.collapse"}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.expand"}}{{/if}}">
						<i class="fas fa-caret-down"></i>
					</button>
					{{!-- The name IS the input, not a label with a pencil beside it: an encounter gets
					      renamed as often as it gets used ("the bridge" becomes "the bridge at dusk"
					      becomes "the ambush"), and a dialog for a one-line edit is a dialog nobody
					      opens. alt+Up / alt+Down in this box reorders the encounter, which is the
					      keyboard half of the grip beside it. --}}
					<input type="text" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-name" value="{{name}}" maxlength="120"
					       aria-label="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.nameLabel"}}">
					<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-count"
					      data-tooltip="{{format "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.countTip" count=entryCount}}"
					      data-tooltip-direction="UP">{{entryCount}}</span>
					<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-tools">
						<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-deploy"
						        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.deploy"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
							<i class="fas fa-map-location-dot"></i>
						</button>
						<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-used"
						        data-tooltip="{{#if used}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.markUnused"}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.markUsed"}}{{/if}}"
						        data-tooltip-direction="UP">
							<i class="fas fa-check"></i>
						</button>
						<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-remove"
						        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.removeEncounter"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
							<i class="fas fa-trash"></i>
						</button>
					</span>
				</div>

				<div class="stonetop-gm-encounter-body">
					{{#if entries.length}}
					<ul class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entries">
						{{#each entries}}
						<li class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry{{#if unresolved}} is-unresolved{{/if}}" data-entry-id="{{id}}">
							<i class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry-icon {{icon}}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
							{{!-- A <button>, not an <a>: an href would make this natively draggable and
							      fight the drag it already emits, and it is a delegated handler either
							      way. See relationships-table.hbs, which says the same thing at
							      length. --}}
							<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry-open"
							        draggable="{{#if unresolved}}false{{else}}true{{/if}}"
							        data-uuid="{{uuid}}" data-doc-type="{{type}}"
							        data-tooltip="{{#if unresolved}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.entryGoneTip"}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.entryOpenTip"}}{{/if}}"
							        data-tooltip-direction="UP">{{name}}</button>
							<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry-kind">{{typeLabel}}</span>
							<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry-remove"
							        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.removeEntry"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
								<i class="fas fa-xmark"></i>
							</button>
							<input type="text" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry-note" value="{{note}}" maxlength="200"
							       placeholder="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.entryNotePlaceholder"}}"
							       aria-label="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.entryNoteLabel"}}">
						</li>
						{{/each}}
					</ul>
					{{/if}}

					{{!-- The visible target for "put it at the END". A drop on a row inserts BEFORE
					      that row, which is what keeps the whole router testable without measuring
					      pointer midpoints — and leaves no way to aim at the bottom of the list
					      except this strip. It doubles as the empty encounter's only content. --}}
					<p class="stonetop-gm-encounter-dropzone">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.dropHere"}}</p>

					<div class="stonetop-gm-encounter-notes-block">
						{{#if notesHtml}}
						<div class="stonetop-gm-encounter-notes">{{{notesHtml}}}</div>
						{{else}}
						<p class="stonetop-gm-encounter-notes stonetop-gm-encounter-notes--empty">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.notesEmpty"}}</p>
						{{/if}}
						{{!-- Read here, written in a pop-up. This sheet re-renders on every prep-page
						      write in the WORLD, and a live ProseMirror on the tab would be torn out
						      from under a GM mid-sentence; a Dialog is a window that redraw cannot
						      reach. See encounter-notes-dialog.js. --}}
						<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-notes-edit"
						        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.notesEdit"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
							<i class="fas fa-pen"></i> {{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.notesEditShort"}}
						</button>
					</div>
				</div>
			</li>
			{{/each}}
		</ul>
		{{else}}
		<p class="stonetop-gm-encounter-empty">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.empty"}}</p>
		{{/if}}

		{{> "stonetop.gm-prep-add-bar" kind="stonetop-gm-encounter"
		    label=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.add")}}

	</section>
</div>
